    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm seeing some really strange spikes in the salinity sensor readings though, especially at the transition layers. They don't seem to follow a smooth gradient and almost look like sensor noise or errors. Do you have any thoughts on why those specific readings might be so erratic or inconsistent?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Erratic salinity spikes in transition layers can often be caused by rapid physical processes, such as brine rejection during intense biological activity or hydrothermal venting mixing with ambient water. Alternatively, it could indicate instrumental issues like biofouling on the sensor or transient electrical noise within the measurement system itself. Cross-referencing these spikes with the temperature and pressure profiles might help distinguish between a real physical event and sensor error.",
      "source": "target",
